{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I understand you’ve been having nausea and vomiting since this morning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, since about 8 AM. I was vomiting every 15 minutes and couldn’t keep anything down. The pain is back too—burning, right here in the upper belly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you had laparoscopic surgery five days ago for a possible hernia?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, they thought it was a paraduodenal hernia, but said they didn’t find one. They fixed a defect in the colon instead."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s right. The CT scan from the ER shows no signs of a surgical complication—no free air, no abscess. The surgeons reviewed it and think this is likely a flare-up of your gastroparesis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not from the surgery? I was worried something went wrong."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, the imaging doesn’t show any issues from the surgery. Your symptoms match what you’ve had before—chronic nausea and delayed stomach emptying. We’re going to admit you for a few days to help control it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. I’ve had this for years. But it’s been worse lately, and I’ve lost weight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s common with gastroparesis. We’ve placed a nasogastric tube to rest your stomach and will give you IV fluids and anti-nausea medication."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The tube is uncomfortable, but I’ll do what I need to. What medicine are you giving me?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2180-01-24 03:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We started IV Zofran, 8 mg. It’s helped many patients like you. We’ll keep monitoring and adjust as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2180-01-24 08:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. How are you feeling this morning?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2180-01-24 08:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. I haven’t vomited since the tube was placed, and the nausea is much less."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2180-01-24 08:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. The chest X-ray confirmed the NG tube is in the right place. We also checked your labs—your potassium is a bit low, so we’re adding some to your IV."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2180-01-24 08:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that why I’ve been feeling weak?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2180-01-24 08:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Low potassium can cause fatigue and muscle weakness. We’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2180-01-24 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The abdominal X-ray shows no obstruction. Air is moving through your bowels, and the NG tube is still in place. This supports that it’s gastroparesis, not a blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2180-01-24 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to know. Can I try drinking something soon?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2180-01-24 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. We want to keep your stomach at rest for now. But you’re on the right track."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2180-01-25 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—your nausea has been gone for over 24 hours. We’re going to stop the NG tube and start you on clear liquids."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2180-01-25 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That’s great! Can I have water or broth?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2180-01-25 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, small sips at first. We’ll see how you tolerate it. If you do well, we’ll advance to a soft diet tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2180-01-25 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And what about my regular meds? I take omeprazole, Celexa, and Synthroid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2180-01-25 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve been giving you those by mouth since yesterday. Your thyroid and acid reflux are stable, so we’re continuing them as before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2180-01-25 13:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your gastroenterologist came by this morning. He agrees this is a gastroparesis flare. He recommends continuing your dromperidone and eating six small, low-fat meals a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2180-01-25 13:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been doing that, but it’s hard to stick to. Are there any other treatments?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2180-01-25 13:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "He mentioned a repeat gastric emptying study could help guide future treatment, but for now, diet and medication are the mainstays."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2180-01-25 13:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll try. My blood pressure has been high lately too—should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2180-01-25 13:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We noticed your diastolic pressure was elevated, but we didn’t start new meds. Your primary doctor should check it after you go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2180-01-25 17:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well on the soft diet. No nausea or vomiting. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2180-01-25 17:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! What should I do when I get home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2180-01-25 17:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue your regular medications. Stick to small, frequent, low-fat meals. Advance your diet slowly as tolerated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2180-01-25 17:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I start feeling sick again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2180-01-25 17:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your gastroenterologist or primary doctor. If it’s severe, go to the ER. But you’re in much better shape now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs this morning are stable. We’ve updated your discharge meds—omeprazole is now 20 mg instead of 40, but everything else is the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that okay? I’ve been on 40 mg for years."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your reflux is well-controlled, and 20 mg is sufficient. It’s a common adjustment."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Alright. And I should follow up with my PCP and gastroenterologist?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. They’ll help manage your gastroparesis long-term. We’ll send them a summary."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel so much better than when I came in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’re discharging you home today. Take it easy, eat small meals, and keep up with your doctors."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks again for your help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing great. Have a safe trip home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad this wasn’t something serious from the surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT and exam confirmed no surgical complications. This was a recurrence of your chronic condition, well-managed now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. I’ll be more careful with my diet going forward."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s the best thing you can do. And reach out if symptoms return—we’re here to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for explaining everything so clearly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we hope not to see you back soon!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2180-01-26 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Same here. Have a good day!"
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2180-01-24 03:01:00",
    "admission_end": "2180-01-26 15:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 55-year-old woman was admitted for recurrent nausea and vomiting five days after laparoscopic repair of a colonic mesenteric defect. Initial evaluation, including CT imaging, ruled out postoperative complications or bowel obstruction. Symptoms were attributed to a flare of chronic gastroparesis. She was managed with NG tube placement, IV fluids, and antiemetics, showing improvement within 24 hours. The NG tube was removed on hospital day two, and her diet was advanced successfully. Her gastroenterologist recommended continued dromperidone and a low-fat, small-meal diet. She was discharged home on day three with stable vital signs, no nausea, and a plan to follow up with her primary care provider and gastroenterologist. No medication changes were made except for reducing omeprazole from 40 mg to 20 mg daily.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Irritable bowel syndrome",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
      "Other postprocedural status"
    ]
  }
}
